WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.710
- [Interviewer] All right, Elmer.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:07.930
Elmer, where'd you grow up?

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:08.763
Where are you from originally?

00:00:08.763 --> 00:00:11.280
- Out there in Eastern Kentucky.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.710
- [Interviewer] This is Leslie County?

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:14.200
- [Elmer] Leslie County, yeah.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.180
- [Interviewer] Tell me
about your childhood.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.790
You had both your parents when you were-

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:17.890
- [Elmer] Yeah, yeah.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.108
- [Interviewer] How was your childhood?

00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:20.865
- There was nine of us.

00:00:20.865 --> 00:00:21.698
- [Interviewer] Nine of you?

00:00:21.698 --> 00:00:22.531
- Yeah.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.617
I'm the baby in the family.

00:00:26.463 --> 00:00:27.940
- [Interviewer] You're the youngest?

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.810
- Youngest one, yeah.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.633
- [Interviewer] How would
you describe your childhood?

00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:33.450
- Growing up pretty rough out there.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.703
Just hard living.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.935
Raised on corn bread and soup beans.

00:00:37.935 --> 00:00:38.768
Potatoes.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:40.663
Garden.

00:00:40.663 --> 00:00:42.870
Always grew a big garden every year.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:43.703
Hogs.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.270
- [Interviewer] You guys have
electricity and running water?

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.823
- No, not for a long time.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.710
Yeah.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.340
- [Interviewer] How far
do you go in school?

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.950
- I went through the ninth grade.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.523
- [Interviewer] Ninth grade.
- Yeah.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:58.943
- [Interviewer] What did you do for work

00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:00.707
after you finished school?

00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:04.285
- Well, I worked, drove a
truck about all my life.

00:01:04.285 --> 00:01:05.785
Hauled coal, logs.

00:01:08.183 --> 00:01:13.110
Been into logging last 10, 15 years.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.050
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.900
Appalachia is a tough
region to survive in, right?

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.510
- Rough.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:20.343
Lots of work.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.100
- [Interviewer] And finding
work, it's a hard thing now.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:23.933
- Yeah.

00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:24.766
- [Interviewer] Coal mine have shut down.

00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:26.210
- You didn't work, you didn't eat.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.380
My grandma always said, she said,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.440
if you gonna eat supper
with me, you gotta work.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.290
If you don't feed nobody, don't work.

00:01:37.321 --> 00:01:41.530
- [Interviewer] So you've
been married, have kids?

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:42.598
- Yeah.

00:01:42.598 --> 00:01:44.730
I got two, boy and a girl.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:45.563
- [Interviewer] And you raised them?

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:46.450
- Yeah.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.067
Yeah, my wife, she passed on.

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:49.293
- [Interviewer] I'm sorry.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.740
So your kids stayed here in-

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.222
- Yes, I raised them out here-

00:01:53.222 --> 00:01:55.620
- [Interviewer] And they
still live in Leslie County?

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.120
- Yeah.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.520
- [Interviewer] Why do
people choose to stay here,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.080
even though there's very few opportunities

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.220
and just such a tough life?

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.090
- This mountain gets to you after a while.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.720
You just don't feel at home nowhere else.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:06.553
- [Interviewer] You go somewhere else

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:07.610
and it just doesn't feel right?

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:08.443
- Yeah.

00:02:08.443 --> 00:02:09.360
- [Interviewer] Yeah, there's
something about this region

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.310
that's very special.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.760
- It's home, you know?

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:12.643
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.170
Drugs have kind of taken over this region.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.424
- Oh, pretty much took over, yeah.

00:02:17.424 --> 00:02:20.223
- [Interviewer] Why do
you think that's happened?

00:02:20.223 --> 00:02:21.056
- Pardon me?

00:02:21.056 --> 00:02:22.740
- [Interviewer] Why do
you think that's happened?

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.071
- Don't know.

00:02:24.071 --> 00:02:24.904
Don't know.

00:02:24.904 --> 00:02:26.460
It's bad, though.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.320
Sure is.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.153
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:02:28.153 --> 00:02:29.223
- Bad, bad situation.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.010
- [Interviewer] You've got
involved in that, as well?

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:32.843
- Huh?

00:02:32.843 --> 00:02:33.676
- [Interviewer] Have you gotten
involved in drugs at all?

00:02:33.676 --> 00:02:34.509
- No.
- [Interviewer] Never?

00:02:34.509 --> 00:02:36.043
- No.
- [Interviewer] Good for you.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.850
What do you think the
future is of this region?

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.290
Because technology marches on
in the rest of the country,

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.850
but this part of the country
is kind of being left behind.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:50.683
- Yeah.

00:02:50.683 --> 00:02:51.516
They'll live on.

00:02:51.516 --> 00:02:52.840
You can't starve these people to death.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.583
They know how to raise
gardens and heel hogs.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.073
Eat squirrels.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.050
- [Interviewer] What do you live on now?

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:04.592
I mean, what's your income?

00:03:04.592 --> 00:03:06.117
- Just disability.

00:03:06.117 --> 00:03:07.507
- [Interviewer] Disability?
- Yeah.

00:03:07.507 --> 00:03:09.693
- [Interviewer] And how
much do you live on a year?

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.843
- I get so much a month.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.070
- [Interviewer] What do
you get a month, roughly?

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.510
- About 1100 a month.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:17.977
I'm on total disability.

00:03:17.977 --> 00:03:19.360
- [Interviewer] And
that's enough to live on?

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.380
- Yeah.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.614
Well, I make do with what the garden grow.

00:03:23.614 --> 00:03:25.300
Still put stuff away.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.450
Doing a lot of canning.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.670
Can a lot of food.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.240
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
but are you happy here?

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.080
- Yeah.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.730
Yeah, ain't no place like home.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.490
- [Interviewer] Yeah, and
you're not looking to leave?

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.320
- No, no.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.069
I like to go on vacation
sometimes over to Florida.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:41.000
- [Interviewer] How far have you traveled?

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.810
- I used to go to Florida
sometimes, about once a year.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:47.030
Take a vacation and
sometimes, Myrtle beach.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:47.863
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:47.863 --> 00:03:50.810
- Since this COVID-19 come
out, I ain't went nowhere.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.130
I stayed pretty much at the house.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:53.033
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.820
Do you have any regrets in your life?

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.440
Do you look back and say
you wish you had done this

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.273
or wish you'd done that?

00:03:59.273 --> 00:04:00.810
- No, no.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:01.643
- [Interviewer] Yeah, you're happy with-

00:04:01.643 --> 00:04:03.303
- I'm happy with, yeah.

00:04:04.368 --> 00:04:05.350
- [Interviewer] What are
the best and worst things

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.277
about living in Appalachia?

00:04:07.277 --> 00:04:11.400
- It's, I guess, I don't know.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.890
I don't say much bad things myself.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.863
I always try to look, at a point

00:04:17.863 --> 00:04:19.840
of looking at the good side.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:20.857
- [Interviewer] Mm-hmm.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.713
- I'm well happy here.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.180
Yeah, it's rough living.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.300
- [Interviewer] It's a
very simple life, though.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.990
- Yeah, you gotta get rough with it.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.380
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.560
You raise your own food.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.375
- Right, I raise a lot
of garden food, yeah.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:37.530
Hogs.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:38.363
- [Interviewer] How old are you?

00:04:38.363 --> 00:04:39.196
- Chickens.

00:04:39.196 --> 00:04:40.423
55.
- [Interviewer] 55.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:43.860
What's the most important lesson

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.073
you've learned in your life?

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:54.623
- Trust in God.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.210
- [Interviewer] You're a religious man?

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.508
- No, but I believe in God.

00:05:01.508 --> 00:05:02.341
- [Interviewer] You don't go to church?

00:05:02.341 --> 00:05:04.424
- No, I did.

00:05:04.424 --> 00:05:05.850
I kinda back slid.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.830
- [Interviewer] So how do you
spend your days, typically?

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:12.513
What do you do?

00:05:12.513 --> 00:05:15.770
- Just worked around
the house, piddle about.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:17.340
You know.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.860
Do a lot of yard work.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.370
Work here and try and get set up,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.000
my trailer set up and everything.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:22.833
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:05:22.833 --> 00:05:24.472
What do your kids do?

00:05:24.472 --> 00:05:26.620
- My boy's a logger.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.130
He logs.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:33.130
And my daughter, I think she
works in the nursing home.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:37.413
Got good kids.

00:05:37.413 --> 00:05:39.860
They ain't old drugs, thank God.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:40.803
- [Interviewer] That's great.

00:05:42.015 --> 00:05:44.880
- Methamphetamines took over this place.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.990
It had, it's ruined all the young people.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.410
Well, not all of them, but a lot of them.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.830
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
it was the pain pills

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.818
and then the crystal meth, yeah.

00:05:54.818 --> 00:05:57.710
- The meth, that nasty
meth they're making.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.980
It's about to do them all in.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:00.813
Sure is.

00:06:00.813 --> 00:06:01.646
- [Interviewer] It almost seems like

00:06:01.646 --> 00:06:03.939
almost the entire community
is affected impacted by it.

00:06:03.939 --> 00:06:06.032
- It's bad, it's bad.

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:06.865
Sure is.

00:06:06.865 --> 00:06:07.970
- [Interviewer] It's rare
to find somebody who isn't.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:08.803
- Yeah.

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:13.660
- [Interviewer] What
opportunities are there

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:16.380
for a person staying,
choosing to stay here?

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:17.213
Coal mines have shut down.

00:06:17.213 --> 00:06:22.110
- Log woods or like Walmart or
somewhere like that, working.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.230
It ain't much.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.480
If you get education,
you have to leave here.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.590
- [Interviewer] And to get real
job, you have to leave here.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:29.423
- Right.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.670
- [Interviewer] But you feel
like you've lived a life

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:34.503
that you wanted.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:37.963
- Yeah.

00:06:37.963 --> 00:06:40.863
I'd like to have got with the
Lord a lot sooner, you know.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.690
It's pretty wild, my childhood days.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:46.710
- [Interviewer] Yeah, what
kind of stuff did you get into?

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.010
- I smoked pot and drunk a lot.

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:50.090
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:50.923
- The drinking, I quit.

00:06:50.923 --> 00:06:52.790
I still smoke a little pot.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.490
I consider it as a herb, not a drug.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.067
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:06:57.067 --> 00:06:58.530
And it's illegal here.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.184
- Oh, yeah.
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:07:00.184 --> 00:07:01.380
- Yeah, they ain't legalized it

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.270
like they have up in California.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:04.103
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:07:04.103 --> 00:07:06.980
For us, it's like, they
don't take it so seriously.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:09.110
- It's a good pain medicine.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:10.540
- [Interviewer] But you live
in this trailer behind you?

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:11.373
- Yeah.

00:07:11.373 --> 00:07:12.206
- [Interviewer] You have electricity now?

00:07:12.206 --> 00:07:13.039
- Yeah.

00:07:13.039 --> 00:07:14.120
- [Interviewer] Do you have running water?

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:15.555
- Yeah.
- [Interviewer] You do.

00:07:15.555 --> 00:07:17.463
- I run it out the holler up there.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.813
Got a waterfall up there it comes off of.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:22.760
- [Interviewer] So you tap into that?

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:23.930
- Yeah.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:25.740
Can't drink it, but I bath in it

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.265
and wash my clothes and dishes.

00:07:28.265 --> 00:07:29.098
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:07:29.098 --> 00:07:30.290
And you have friends?

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:31.123
- Huh?

00:07:31.123 --> 00:07:31.956
- [Interviewer] You have friends?

00:07:31.956 --> 00:07:32.789
- Oh, yeah.

00:07:32.789 --> 00:07:33.622
Yeah, I got friends.

00:07:33.622 --> 00:07:34.693
Not many, but a few.

00:07:35.852 --> 00:07:36.685
You know.

00:07:36.685 --> 00:07:37.518
- [Interviewer] You don't need a lot.

00:07:37.518 --> 00:07:38.703
I stay a lot to myself.

00:07:40.467 --> 00:07:42.183
I'm a by myself person.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.740
I've got a wife, but she
lives in another home.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:47.830
I live here.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.337
- [Interviewer] I understand.

00:07:49.337 --> 00:07:50.730
And do you hunt?

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:51.563
- Yeah.

00:07:51.563 --> 00:07:52.770
- [Interviewer] What kinda
animals do you hunt for?

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.043
- Deer, squirrel, rabbit.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:57.453
Snakes.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.550
- [Interviewer] Do you have bear here?

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:01.383
- Yeah.

00:08:01.383 --> 00:08:02.940
One comes in here every night.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:03.773
- [Interviewer] Really?

00:08:03.773 --> 00:08:04.680
- Yeah, got one coming.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:05.513
A big bear.

00:08:05.513 --> 00:08:06.600
He swooped my bulldog.

00:08:08.492 --> 00:08:10.159
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.600
- He tore him up pretty bad last time.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:16.487
Cut him up.

00:08:17.394 --> 00:08:19.177
He'll get after you.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.690
- [Interviewer] So living up
here all by yourself, Elmer,

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:26.643
is there anything that you
do to protect yourself?

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.590
- Well, I've got a pistol I pack with me.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:31.423
- [Interviewer] Oh, is that right?

00:08:31.423 --> 00:08:33.113
- Yeah, I keep it for the bears.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.593
The bear's bad to come in on
me out there in the night.

00:08:38.593 --> 00:08:41.096
- [Interviewer] How close
do the bears get to you?

00:08:41.096 --> 00:08:43.940
- They've come stuck their
head through the door at me.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.120
- [Interviewer] They
come into your trailer?

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.190
- Yeah, I left the door open one night

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.300
and there's one come stuck
its head through the door,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:51.133
looked at me.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:53.606
- [Interviewer] That's where
your gun comes in handy.

00:08:53.606 --> 00:08:54.475
- Yeah.

00:08:54.475 --> 00:08:55.308
(interviewer laughing)

00:08:55.308 --> 00:08:56.257
I had a pistol.

00:08:56.257 --> 00:08:59.740
I got a rifle and I keep it for snakes

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:05.059
and people that's mean to
me, tries to be mean to him.

00:09:05.059 --> 00:09:06.780
I ain't able to fight them.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.090
I'd have to shoot them.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.540
It just rough living
here in the mountains.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:13.040
You've gotta protect yourself.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.630
Snakes is bad and the birds is bad.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:18.507
Some big cats running around here

00:09:19.993 --> 00:09:22.893
and some mean people running around here.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.563
But they don't bother me any.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:30.330
I guess, for I got a gun
on me, I ain't no better.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.043
- [Interviewer] They don't mess with you?

00:09:32.043 --> 00:09:34.240
- No, they don't mess for me.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.123
I don't mess with nobody, neither.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.043
My grandpa, he was a doctor,
a lawyer, undertaker,

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.060
and he'd taken the sick people

00:09:50.446 --> 00:09:52.835
and he'd take and nurse them to health.

00:09:52.835 --> 00:09:57.002
And if they died, he would
take and build a coffin

00:09:57.978 --> 00:10:00.728
and preach the funeral bury them.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.280
He did have some slaves,
back in his day and time.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:06.113
- [Interviewer] He had slaves?

00:10:06.113 --> 00:10:06.946
- Yeah.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.468
And my grandma out there, she
raised kids up there and stuff

00:10:11.468 --> 00:10:14.980
and she breastfed them
and panthers would come

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.210
and try to claw in on her.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.423
They'd stick their paws through the door.

00:10:19.423 --> 00:10:22.733
She'd chop their paws off of a hatchet.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.051
- [Interviewer] This is your grandparents?

00:10:26.051 --> 00:10:29.250
- Yeah, they had it rough back then,

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.600
but the slaves, they'd grow
corn, feed the animals with.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:33.963
They had mules, oxes.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:39.271
My grandpa was a cranky old dude, but-

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:41.260
- [Interviewer] So he was a doctor?

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:42.240
- A lawyer.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:43.073
- [Interviewer] Lawyer.

00:10:43.073 --> 00:10:45.040
- Undertaker and a preacher.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:45.873
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:10:45.873 --> 00:10:47.780
- He done a little bit of all that

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.150
and up there where he
lived is a big log house

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:53.781
and he buried all of them
out in the bottom out there,

00:10:53.781 --> 00:10:55.520
all them people that
would die, he'd bury them

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.210
and make graves that
ain't never been marked.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:01.483
I'd say 50, 60 graves up there
that ain't never been marked.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.330
He's a go-getter, that old man was.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.820
Jake Napper was his name
and he was out there.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:14.970
He'd whoop you.

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:17.100
When I was a kid that there,
if you killed a piss-ant

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.020
on his farm, you was whooped.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.470
He called them little people.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:22.760
He didn't believe in killing nothing

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.160
unless you was gonna eat it.

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:27.870
And that pretty much covered that.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:30.403
- [Interviewer] All right, Elmer.

00:11:30.403 --> 00:11:31.790
Well, thank you so much
for talking with me.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:32.623
- [Elmer] Yes, sir.

00:11:32.623 --> 00:11:33.500
- [Interviewer] Good luck with everything

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:34.810
and the bears, too.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:35.760
- [Elmer] Oh, yeah.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:38.043
I'll tame the bears.

00:11:38.043 --> 00:11:40.293
(laughing)

